WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:05.140
B.J. Novak played Dunder Mifflin employee
Ryan Howard during The Office's long network

00:00:05.140 --> 00:00:09.120
run, but he hasn't latched onto an equally
high-profile project since the series left

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:09.900
the air.

00:00:09.900 --> 00:00:12.500
That doesn't mean he's been sitting idle,
however.

00:00:12.500 --> 00:00:14.340
This is what B.J. Novak has been up to.

00:00:14.520 --> 00:00:19.760
While he definitely isn't appearing on TV
quite as much, B.J. Novak never left television.

00:00:19.760 --> 00:00:24.700
The year after The Office ended, Novak had
a cameo on the comedy Community and a recurring

00:00:24.710 --> 00:00:29.499
role on Aaron Sorkin's The Newsroom playing
Lucas Pruitt, the owner of the company airing

00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:30.320
News Night.

00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:34.880
Along with a bit of voice work for the animated
show Arthur, Novak enjoyed a recurring role

00:00:34.880 --> 00:00:39.500
as Mindy's ex-boyfriend Jamie on The Mindy
Project and played a satirical version of

00:00:39.500 --> 00:00:44.180
himself on two episodes of the musical romantic
comedy series Crazy Ex-Girlfriend.

00:00:44.540 --> 00:00:48.200
"Well… back to the old ecstasy factory!"

00:00:48.360 --> 00:00:53.060
Novak is also doing a lot more work behind
the camera, particularly in terms of his work

00:00:53.060 --> 00:00:54.300
on The Mindy Project.

00:00:54.300 --> 00:00:59.200
Just as he did for The Office, Novak has worked
as writer, director, and producer on The Mindy

00:00:59.210 --> 00:01:03.450
Project, which isn't surprising considering
his long history with the show's creator and

00:01:03.450 --> 00:01:04.440
star, Mindy Kaling.

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:10.040
B.J. Novak was showing interest in more film
work long before The Office shut its doors,

00:01:10.040 --> 00:01:15.040
winning small roles in 2007's comedy Knocked
Up, the buddy drama Reign Over Me released

00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:20.470
the same year, the 2012 political satire The
Dictator, and most visibly as one of the few

00:01:20.470 --> 00:01:25.680
lucky survivors of Quentin Tarantino's 2009
bloodbath Inglourious Basterds.

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:30.300
With the exception of the series finale, Novak
wasn't around for The Office's last season,

00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:32.860
and his film credits are probably a big clue
as to why.

00:01:32.860 --> 00:01:38.000
Novak was busy in 2013, The Office's final
year, doing more work on the big screen.

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:42.000
He reprised his role as Baker Smurf in 2013's
The Smurfs 2.

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:46.340
That year he also had a small role in the
comedy The Internship and a meatier role in

00:01:46.340 --> 00:01:48.580
the comedy-drama Saving Mr. Banks.

00:01:48.580 --> 00:01:53.979
The latter film revolves around the development
of 1964's classic Mary Poppins; Novak plays

00:01:53.980 --> 00:01:58.720
songwriter Robert Sherman who, with brother
Richard Sherman, co-wrote the film's songs.

00:01:58.720 --> 00:02:04.220
The following year Novak played Alistair Smythe
in Amazing Spider-Man 2, a character who may

00:02:04.229 --> 00:02:09.349
have eventually gotten some spotlight if that
franchise hadn't died, Smythe is the supervillain

00:02:09.349 --> 00:02:11.620
called the Spider Slayer in the comics.

00:02:11.620 --> 00:02:16.299
More recently, Novak appeared in 2017's The
Founder, about the creation of McDonald's,

00:02:16.300 --> 00:02:21.080
as Harry J. Sonneborn, the McDonald's Corporation's
first president and CEO.

00:02:21.320 --> 00:02:26.880
In 2013, B.J. Novak signed a two-book deal
with publisher Alfred A. Knopf, and the following

00:02:26.889 --> 00:02:31.489
year his literary career premiered with the
short story collection One More Thing: Stories

00:02:31.489 --> 00:02:32.700
and Other Stories.

00:02:32.700 --> 00:02:37.140
The book enjoyed a six-week stay on New York
Times' Hardcover Fiction Bestseller List.

00:02:37.360 --> 00:02:40.420
"The concept is that I wrote a book, so now
I'm all pretentious."

00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:42.700
"People think you're pretentious anyway."

00:02:42.920 --> 00:02:47.560
The Washington Post's Jen Chaney described
Novak's writing as being gifted with a style

00:02:47.680 --> 00:02:52.919
"...part Steven Wright and part Charlie Kaufman,
married with a sharp ear for (and satire of)

00:02:52.920 --> 00:02:54.240
contemporary pop culture."

00:02:54.440 --> 00:02:58.060
The next book B.J. Novak wrote targeted an
entirely different audience.

00:02:58.060 --> 00:03:03.460
Released after One More Thing: Stories and
Other Stories in 2014, his children's book

00:03:03.460 --> 00:03:08.540
The Book With No Pictures spent 55 consecutive
weeks on the New York Times Picture Book Bestseller

00:03:08.540 --> 00:03:09.280
List.

00:03:09.280 --> 00:03:13.320
If you're wondering how a children's book
that advertises it doesn't have pictures might

00:03:13.320 --> 00:03:15.100
appeal to children, well…

00:03:15.420 --> 00:03:16.680
"BLORK!

00:03:20.220 --> 00:03:21.180
Wait, what?

00:03:22.240 --> 00:03:23.360
That doesn't even mean anything!"

00:03:23.620 --> 00:03:28.559
The beginning of the book explains, quote,
"Everything the words say, the person reading

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:29.980
the book has to say.

00:03:29.980 --> 00:03:30.900
No matter what."

00:03:30.900 --> 00:03:35.860
The rest of the book, in large colored letters,
then forces the presumably adult reader to

00:03:35.870 --> 00:03:41.189
say ridiculous things that get the kids laughing
like, quote, "I am a monkey who taught myself

00:03:41.189 --> 00:03:45.220
to read," or, quote, "My head is made of blueberry
pizza."

00:03:45.820 --> 00:03:51.200
In May 2015, news broke that B.J. Novak and
Mindy Kaling were going to collaborate on

00:03:51.209 --> 00:03:55.099
yet another project, and this one wasn't going
to be on television.

00:03:55.099 --> 00:03:59.939
The same month The Mindy Project was canceled
by Fox and subsequently picked up by Hulu,

00:03:59.939 --> 00:04:05.370
the New York Daily News reported that Novak
and Kaling had signed a $7.5 million deal

00:04:05.370 --> 00:04:08.150
to write a book "about their off-and-on romance."

00:04:08.150 --> 00:04:12.230
The following month, Kaling talking to Vanity
Fair about the deal, saying that she and Novak

00:04:12.230 --> 00:04:15.000
didn't, quote, "know all that much about it"
yet.

00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:18.539
She explained that they were still in the
early stages with the book and that they weren't

00:04:18.539 --> 00:04:22.230
completely sure where they were going with
it save for the fact that, according to Kaling,

00:04:22.230 --> 00:04:24.380
it would be, quote, "great and funny."

00:04:24.380 --> 00:04:28.620
In spite of what some may have assumed from
the New York Daily News story, however, Kaling

00:04:28.629 --> 00:04:32.410
said that while she and Novak would be talking
about their romantic relationship as well

00:04:32.410 --> 00:04:35.160
as their friendship, that the book, quote,
"won't be a tell-all."

00:04:35.160 --> 00:04:39.040
So far, nothing has materialized as a result
of the deal, but keep your eyes on your local

00:04:39.040 --> 00:04:40.040
bookstore.

00:04:40.600 --> 00:04:47.560
After he appeared as McDonald's first president
and CEO Harry J. Sonneborn in 2017's The Founder,

00:04:47.560 --> 00:04:52.860
in a totally Ryan Howard-esque move, Novak
decided to make life imitate art.

00:04:52.860 --> 00:04:57.900
Just like his Founder character, Novak is
hoping to become an innovation supporter…of

00:04:57.900 --> 00:04:58.700
meat.

00:04:58.700 --> 00:05:01.200
Novak told WWD's Kristen Tauer:

00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:02.789
"This sounds sci-fi.

00:05:02.789 --> 00:05:07.160
Do you know about the companies that are using
meat stem cells to clone meat in a lab?"

00:05:07.160 --> 00:05:11.500
Novak went on to describe the process, saying
it was possible to take the cell of an animal

00:05:11.500 --> 00:05:13.980
and, quote, "grow and grow and grow it."

00:05:13.980 --> 00:05:18.020
Meaning that, as opposed to the high numbers
of animals butchered for meat now, quote,

00:05:18.020 --> 00:05:21.300
"you could just get one unlucky cow and feed
the world forever."

00:05:21.300 --> 00:05:25.980
Novak said he supports a number of companies
doing the work and singled out Memphis Meats

00:05:25.990 --> 00:05:27.000
as one of them.

00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:29.040
Man, Dwight would be so jealous…

00:05:29.520 --> 00:05:31.920
"I can get you exotic meats.

00:05:31.920 --> 00:05:34.900
Hippo steaks, giraffe burgers."

00:05:35.840 --> 00:05:36.880
"We'll talk."

